 ],
 "literal": "You are sorrowful, may you live, bewildering evening! Like my lover, is it hard-hearted, your companion?",
 "translation": "May you live long, O bewildering evening! You look so sorrowful. Is your companion too as hard-hearted as my lover?",
 "modern": "மயக்கம் தரும் மாலையே! நீ வாழ்க! நீ துன்பத்துடன் காணப்படுகிறாய். என் காதலனைப் போல உன்னுடைய துணையும் இரக்கமற்ற நெஞ்சத்தைக் கொண்டிருக்கிறதா?",
 "explanation": "The heroine, suffering from the pain of separation, addresses the evening twilight. She notices that the evening seems dim and sorrowful, just like her. She asks the evening if its own companion, the sun which has departed, is as cruel and hard-hearted as her own lover who has left her. The word 'puṉkaṇ' (sorrow) denotes the sad appearance of the evening, while 'vaṉkaṇ' (hard-heartedness) highlights the perceived cruelty of the departing companion.",
